# G015 — Dijkstra's Algorithm

*v2. See `dijkstra.md` for prose: a dependency graph IS a
choreography, weighted edges are costs (time, money, compute), the
resolver could pick the cheapest resolution path, `<-` gate
pipelines are Dijkstra applied to verification cost.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Cheapest Agent Route

[cheapest-route @goal
    IN ORDER TO
        given multiple agents who can fulfil @goal with different
        cost profiles, pick the route with the minimum total cost
        so the choreography is optimised across concurrent alternatives

    @graph <- [registry[agent-graph
                           {goal: @goal,
                            cost-metric: "seconds"}]]

    @route <- [dijkstra
                 {graph: @graph, start: "dispatcher", goal: @goal}]

    @route
        where distance at most @budget-seconds
            -> for @node in @route:path
                [dispatch[step {agent: @node}]]
        otherwise
            -> #no-route-within-budget
]

---

## Verb Surface

[dijkstra
    shortest-path        {graph, start, goal}    ;; -> {distance, path}
    all-distances        {graph, start}           ;; -> {node → distance}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: pipeline ordering as `where` cheapest-first

[gate-pipeline {gates: @gates, input: @in}]
    IN ORDER TO
        run gates in ascending-cost order so early rejection saves work
        same principle Dijkstra gives for graph traversal

    @gates ordered by estimated-cost ascending
    for @g in @gates
        where @g(@in) is true -> continue to next gate
        otherwise              -> reject early (#gate-failed @g)
]

Verification pipelines ARE shortest-path problems in disguise. The
`<-` gate sequence is a path through the space of validation steps;
cheapest-first is the Dijkstra-optimal ordering.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: resolver chooses path `where` multiple resolutions exist

[resolve {target: @t, strategy: "dijkstra"}]
    when @t has multiple resolution paths
    the resolver builds a graph of (handler → cost) edges
    and returns the result from the cheapest path
    IN ORDER TO
        pick the cheapest fulfilment when competing agents could all
        produce @t, so cost and latency are the tie-breakers
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @g <- {"A": [("B", 4), ("C", 2)],
           "B": [("D", 3), ("E", 1)],
           "C": [("B", 1), ("D", 5)],
           "D": [("E", 2), ("F", 6)],
           "E": [("F", 3)]}

    -> [dijkstra[shortest-path
                    {graph: @g, start: "A", goal: "F"}]]
    ;; -> {distance: 7, path: ["A", "C", "B", "E", "F"]}

    -> [dijkstra[all-distances
                    {graph: @g, start: "A"}]]
    ;; -> {"A": 0, "B": 3, "C": 2, "D": 6, "E": 4, "F": 7}
]
